==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|wait upon}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈweɪt ˌɑn/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈweɪt ˌɒn/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-wait on.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# To provide a [[service]] to (someone); to act as a [[servant]] to (someone); to serve (someone) as a [[waiter]] or [[waitress]] in a restaurant.
#: {{syn|en|attend to|Thesaurus:serve}}
#: {{ux|en|Is someone '''waiting on''' you yet?}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|I|1|text=How now, Simple! where have you been? I must '''wait on''' myself, must I?}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|II|I|page=6|text={{...}} for such a large house there are very few servants, because master has never lived here much; and when he does come, being a bachelor, he needs little '''waiting on''':}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1942|author=w:Zora Neale Hurston|title=w:Dust Tracks on a Road|location=New York|publisher=Arno Press|year_published=1969|chapter=9|page=171|url=https://archive.org/details/dusttracksonroad00hurs/page/171
|passage=“Run on, fellow. You can’t get '''waited on''' in here.”<br>“I’ll stay right here until I do. I know my rights. {{...}} I’ll get '''waited on''' all right, or sue the place.”}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} To [[wait for]] (a person).
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''waiting on''' you before we can leave.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|27|14|text='''Wait on''' the L<small>ORD</small>: be of good courage, and he shall strengthen thine heart:}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|6|letter=26|page=89|text=Be pleased to write to me by Rogers; who will '''wait on''' you for your answer, at your own time.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} To [[wait for]] (a thing, or an event to take place).
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''waiting on''' the light to change.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Far from the Madding Crowd|II|Oak's Advancement— A Great Hope|page=223|text={{...}} a time was coming {{...}} when his '''waiting on''' events should have its reward.}}
#* {{RQ:Kipling Kim|chapter=13|page=340|text={{...}} with grass mats over their heads and the raindrops puddling in their footprints, [they] '''waited on''' the weather.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author={{w|Daphne Du Maurier}}|chapter={{w|The Birds (story)|The Birds}}|title=Stories of Suspense|location=New York|publisher=Scholastic Book Services|year_published=1972|page=17|url=https://archive.org/details/storiesofsuspens00mace/page/16
|passage=It was as though they [the gulls] '''waited upon''' some signal; as though some decision had yet to be given.}}
# To be [[in store]] for (someone).
#: {{syn|en|await}}
#* {{RQ:Burke Regicide Peace|letter=1|page=62|text=the destruction that '''waits on''' Monarchs, who consider fidelity to them as the most degrading of all vices}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1969|author=w:Maya Angelou|title=w:I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings|url=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780812980028/page/9|chapter=1|pages=9–10|publisher=Random House|location=New York
|passage=Their wages wouldn’t even get them out of debt to my grandmother, not to mention the staggering bill that '''waited on''' them at the white commissary downtown.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[visit]] (someone) as a [[courtesy]], on business or for ceremony.
#: {{syn|en|attend|call on}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|2|7|13|page=68|text=In the Evening our Commander sent a Message to Mr. ''Jones,'' that if a Visit would not be troublesome he would '''wait on''' him.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|1|6|page=42|text=The ladies of Longbourn soon '''waited on''' those of Netherfield. The visit was returned in due form.}}
#* {{RQ:Douglass Bondage|chapter=25|page=399|passage={{...}} I went, as my custom was, into one of the best railroad carriages on the road. {{...}} I was soon '''waited upon''' by the conductor, and ordered out;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|author=w:Robert Byron|title=w:The Road to Oxiana|location=New York|publisher=Oxford University Press|year_published=1982|section=Part 3, p. 114|url=https://archive.org/details/roadtooxiana00byro_0/page/114
|passage=Lest [my resolve] should break down, I took a stiff dose of whisky and '''waited on''' the Governor early.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To occur as a consequence of (something), to [[result]] from (something).
#* {{RQ:Allestree Decay|3|49|text=that ruine which '''waits upon''' such a supine temper}}
#* {{RQ:Congreve Mourning Bride|page=66|text=For Blessings ever '''wait on''' virtuous Deeds;<br>And tho’ a late, a sure Reward succeeds.}}
#* {{RQ:Byron Childe Harold|2|98|118|text=What is the worst of woes that '''wait on''' age?}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[carry out]] or [[perform]] (a [[duty]], [[function]], etc.).
#: {{syn|en|attend to}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Numbers|3|10|text=Aaron and his sons {{...}} shall '''wait on''' their priest’s office:}}
# {{lb|en|falconry|of a hawk}} To fly above its master, waiting till game is sprung.
# {{lb|en|colloquial|United States|archaic}} To [[court]] (someone).
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[obey]] and [[pay attention]] to (someone); to follow (a leader); to [[attend to]] (someone); to be subject to (someone).

